  geopolitics: "The transmission is now running firmly in the de-escalation direction and it is the dominant fact on this board, because it is simultaneously setting the terms of trade for two commodity currencies and hollowing out the inflation case under two central banks. The supply side of the Iran conflict has normalised faster than this page assumed a week ago. CENTCOM says it has supported more than a billion barrels of crude leaving the Gulf through the Strait of Hormuz in roughly two months and assisted over 2,000 commercial ship transits, its commander Admiral Brad Cooper says the primary transit lanes are clear of mines after US Navy underwater drones identified more than 100 suspected devices in the Traffic Separation Scheme that contractors then dealt with, and crude and LNG flows through the strait have reached a six-month high. Middle East oil flows have averaged 17.1 million barrels a day over the past ten days even though the Saudi East-West pipeline remains disrupted after the attacks, which is the specific detail that matters: the system is now routing around the damage rather than being constrained by it, exactly as this page conceded yesterday when it marked its own Rapidan-based call wrong. Saudi Arabia moved 2.8 million barrels a day through Hormuz in the six days to September 18 against 700,000 a day in August and Aramco is guiding to full pipeline operation within six weeks. Alongside the supply repair sits diplomacy with a date on it. Trump reportedly decided against bombing Yemen for now, has opened back-channel talks with the Houthis whose representatives say they do not intend to attack American vessels, and has said he is probably open to meeting President Pezeshkian at a UN General Assembly running September 22 to 26 and again on the 28th, where Pezeshkian will address the chamber and hold sideline talks. The price has done the rest: WTI is 93.388, down 11.76% on the week after four consecutive losing sessions, Brent is back under 102 at 101.661 and down 6.52%, and the WTI-Brent spread at 8.27 dollars is the widest this page has carried, which itself argues the US benchmark has overshot. Gas has gone with it - EU gas 73.83 and -7.78%, German gas -11.32%, UK gas 184.91 and -6.80%. The FX read has four legs and they should be held together rather than separately. First, Canada: the entire terms-of-trade support under the loonie has gone, and Macklem handed the market the conditional that makes it explicit, saying inflation would be expected to edge up 'if oil prices stay near US$100 per barrel' and that the Bank will not let energy effects broaden into persistent inflation - a hawkish clause written at a price that no longer exists. CAD holds -1 and USD/CAD at 1.40250 is the widest gap on the page. Second, Australia, and this is the leg most likely to be missed: Bullock's case for hiking into a supply shock rests on second-round effects from higher-for-longer oil, and she restated it this morning on the fourth day of an 11.76% crude decline. A near fully priced hike resting on a deflating premise is the most asymmetric position on the board, which is why AUD holds +2 but nothing Australian is upgraded. Third, the havens: gold has lost its geopolitical leg and is held up only by record ETF holdings of 4,189 tonnes, while the franc has weakened through a shooting war with USD/CHF +2.21% on the month - which also removes the pressure the SNB wrote its intervention language for, two days before it has to repeat or revise it. Fourth, equities: falling Brent, German gas down 11.32% and a Bund easing to 3.4536% are the reason the DAX closed 1.07% higher at 25,575 on Monday and trades 25,640 now, straight through the worst CDU state result in postwar German history, and the reason GER40 turns LONG. The risk to all four is the same single risk and it is concentrated rather than diversified: Trump has threatened to wipe Iran out or let its economy rot absent a deal, the Saudi pipeline is still damaged, and speculation about exactly this meeting circulated at last year's assembly and came to nothing. One re-escalation headline out of New York this week moves USOIL, USD/CAD, CAD/CHF, XAU and GER40 against this page simultaneously.",

